{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792714349,"nonce":"LamjWLgyt7AWPASeg1LPEDOF99o=","message":"The idle sources were the Workbench LSP timer (200 ms with open documents), the native LSP writer recv_timeout (100 ms), and the Studio extension supervisor (500 ms once quiet). LSP pipes and extension VM output now signal the platform wake primitive on an empty-to-nonempty queue transition. Writer waits block until input or an explicit close sentinel. Per-tree flush hooks drain queued events; timers remain for delayed replies, reload debounce, initialization, request timeouts, restart and shutdown. Adapters without a wake primitive retain polling.\n\nValidation: 21 native Rust tests pass; LSP protocol/lifecycle checks pass (concurrent out-of-order responses, cancellation, timeout, crash/replay, restart and stop); native delayed-LSP notification and mounted-extension view tests pass without poll timers. LSP UI checks cover the welcome page, settled code, fallback polling, documentless requests, last/bulk close and explicit restart.\n\nFinal native AOT run against an isolated Haxe workspace with the bundled Rayzor extension, after startup, measured 0.0% then 0.1% CPU in top. The loop had no periodic half-second supervision turns and slept through the idle interval until the bounded test shutdown. The initial animation frames are recorded separately; this does not assert that a focused blinking caret never renders. Artifacts: bin/lsp-idle-aot-final.log and bin/lsp-idle-aot-final.top.txt. The rebuilt AOT RayDE.app passes strict codesign verification and its six-second launch/exit smoke test.","files":null}]}